C has 7 decimals). Times are ledger timestamps in seconds."],[262,"This removes a whole class of mistakes and attacks: a mistyped address, a pasted address from a phishing message, or an address invented by a language model cannot be expressed as a payment."],[335,"Transaction authorization"],[61,"Transfer testnet USDC from Freighter into the team-account contract."],[151,"Transfer, then the `pay` receipt event"],[121,"Two Soroban contracts make up EcoRouter on Stellar. Both live in the repository under `contracts/`."],[116,"Types"],[27,"USDC amounts passed as strings are decimal USDC values with at most seven fractional places. A `bigint` is already in the token's base units. `scheduleKey` returns a SHA-256 hex digest of `ecorouter:v1:${team}:${agent}:${service}:${slot}`."],[350,"USDC is transferred and a receipt event is emitted."],[17,"Use from any language"],[299,"Verified on chain"],[115,"Views"],[410,"What a policy holds"],[2,"What an agent key can do, what the policy limits, and what remains outside the contract."],[256,"What can happen to a service"],[370,"What cannot be approved"],[220,"What if a payment is refused?"],[392,"What the assistant cannot do"],[296,"What we built"],[432,"When a payment is refused"],[372,"When a person's payment breaks one of four rules, it is **held**: the service is not on their approved list, the amount is over the cap per payment, they have used up their payments for the period, or the amount is over the allowance left. Nothing moves. The payment is not lost either: the spender can ask the owner for an exception to that one payment."],[219,"Where do I get the contract ids?"],[257,"Who controls what"],[390,"Why a hallucinated recipient cannot happen"],[255,"Why payments take an id, not an address"],[391,"Why the amount comes from code"],[25,"With no subcommand, the same binary starts the scheduled worker described in The worker."],[94,"withdraw funds,"],[298,"Written for this build"],[223,"Yes. Use Freighter connected to Stella